{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:8d6487600263278b63ec8691777c2d5d153b7d0c9ea82f778b8f5d65701321b7","modelForecastIds":["sha256:ce5ac6903f2f113f94bdb399ba043b601c3519ebe168d5f451c2c9fb553bdd6c"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.15},{"probability":0.5,"value":2},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.95}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"DECOMPOSITION: y = r* + real term premium - repression wedge, G7 GDP-weighted, 2032-36 average.\nWEIGHTS FIRST, because this is the step most forecasters skip. G7 GDP weights at market rates: US ~0.48, Germany ~0.11, Japan ~0.10, UK ~0.09, France ~0.08, Italy ~0.06, Canada ~0.06. Japan's and Germany's structurally low real yields drag the aggregate FAR below the US number, so the question is NOT a question about TIPS even though the stated starting point is TIPS.\nCOMPONENT 1, r* = 1.00% (80% range 0.3-1.8). 2026 base: US ~1.3, euro area ~0.5, Japan ~0.15, UK ~1.0, France ~0.5, Italy ~0.3, Canada ~1.5; GDP-weighted = 0.48(1.3)+0.11(0.5)+0.10(0.15)+0.09(1.0)+0.08(0.5)+0.06(0.3)+0.06(1.5) = 0.624+0.055+0.015+0.090+0.040+0.018+0.090 = 0.93%. Changes to 2032-36, signed and sized: life-cycle DISSAVING (US 1946-64 cohort aged 68-90 in the window, peak decumulation) +25 bps, NETTED against the longevity/life-cycle saving leg that Auclert-type work finds still dominant -20 bps, so pure demography on r* = +5 bps only; public dissaving and rollover volume (gross public debt past 100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US / ~127% China by 2031) +35 bps incremental to what the 2026 level already prices; AI/grid capex demand for loanable funds = $0.7-1.1T/yr against world gross saving ~$25T, i.e. a 3-4% claim, worth +25 bps IF it persists, but haircut by AI-Q4's 66% impairment marginal (post-bust excess capacity means no new funding demand in the 2032-36 window) to 0.34(25) + 0.66(2) = +10 bps; precautionary saving under labour-income uncertainty, weighted by AI-Q5 at 38%, = 0.38(-40) + 0.62(-8) = -20 bps; cognitive-labour compression depressing household income and consumption (BIS 2026 Box C) an additional -10 bps; Chinese and OECD savings-glut recycling, with China still exporting $500-800B/yr of saving at a 2-3%-of-GDP surplus through the window, -20 bps. NET CHANGE = +5 +35 +10 -20 -10 -20 = 0 bps. r* is roughly FLAT, which is the point: I set 1.00%.\nTHIS IS WHERE I RESPECT THE 2A DISTINCTION. Section 2A says aging worsens global (r - g) by ~+1.0 pp EVEN WHERE a frictionless r* eases. My decomposition honours that literally: the r* leg nets to zero-to-easing, and the +1.0 pp lives (a) in g falling - my CQ3 of 1.7%/yr is ~1.1 pp below the no-aging counterfactual of ~2.56%, which IS the claim - and (b) in the term premium and fiscal arithmetic below. I do not smuggle the (r-g) deterioration into r*.\nCOMPONENT 2, real term premium = +130 bps (80% range 60-220). 2026 base reconstructed from the locked observation: US 10y TIPS at 2.60% (DFII10, Oct 30 2026) against a US r* of ~1.3 and an expected average short real rate over 10 years of ~1.5 implies ~110 bps of US real term premium already. G7-weighted 2026: 0.48(110)+0.11(50)+0.10(30)+0.09(120)+0.08(70)+0.06(140)+0.06(90) = 52.8+5.5+3.0+10.8+5.6+8.4+5.4 = 91.5 bps. Drivers to 2032-36: rising gross issuance/GDP and rollover volume; net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP; eroding sovereign convenience yields (IMF Fiscal Monitor April 2026); levered hedge-fund basis-trade absorption as a FRAGILE marginal buyer, which is a dealer-balance-sheet term premium that widens discontinuously rather than smoothly; 3-yr P95 Debt-at-Risk of 117% of GDP, +2.4 pp more under a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock; and indexation/inflation-uncertainty risk in the real leg. Central +130, i.e. +38 bps of widening.\nVARIANCE ATTRIBUTION (the requirement). The term premium's 80% range (160 bps) and r*'s (150 bps) are comparable in UNCONDITIONAL width, but essentially all of the variance that is CORRELATED WITH THE DEMOGRAPHICS x AI COUPLING sits in the term premium, because the coupling acts through issuance volume, convenience yield, and marginal-buyer fragility - all term-premium objects. r*'s variance is largely orthogonal (it turns on a contested life-cycle saving elasticity that aging moves in both directions at once). The term premium therefore carries most of my answer's conditional variance; the repression wedge carries the skew.\nCOMPONENT 3, repression wedge = -30 bps (range -10 to -90), treated as a PRICED POLICY OPTION, not a tail. Definition: bps suppressed below the free-market level by YCC, QE, LCR/Solvency-II/pension captive demand, or issuance-maturity shortening. Japan already runs one: BoJ holdings still ~45% of JGBs, so Japan's weight 0.10 times an existing ~100 bps wedge = 10 bps is already baked into the aggregate. For the US/euro area/UK (combined weight ~0.78): P(an episode of explicit or quasi-explicit curve management or new captive-demand mandate at some point in 2032-36) = 45%, justified by the Debt-at-Risk arithmetic rather than by precedent - at 142% US debt/GDP and 3%-of-GDP net interest, a 100 bps rise in the average funding cost costs ~1.4% of GDP per year in steady state, which exceeds the political tolerance for any discretionary program of comparable size. Conditional window-averaged wedge ~60 bps on the affected national yields: 0.45 x 60 x 0.78 = 21 bps, plus Japan's 10 = ~30 bps expected.\nSUM: y = 1.00 + 1.30 - 0.30 = 2.00% central.\nMEAN-REVERSION ASSUMPTION MADE EXPLICIT, since the brief says it matters more than the level. Back out the implied US path: if G7 = 2.00 and the non-US legs contribute 0.10(0.7)+0.11(1.3)+0.08(1.5)+0.06(2.4)+0.09(2.3)+0.06(2.0) = 0.07+0.14+0.12+0.14+0.21+0.12 = 0.80, then the US must supply 1.20 at weight 0.48, i.e. a US 10y real of 2.50% averaged over 2032-36 against 2.60% observed on Oct 30 2026. So my central case assumes essentially NO mean reversion - about 10 bps - because every force I sized above (public dissaving, rollover volume, convenience-yield erosion, marginal-buyer fragility) is secular rather than cyclical, and the two components that would pull it back down (precautionary saving, savings glut) are the two I sized smallest with the least confidence.\nP10 = 1.15: r* eases to ~0.45 (the longevity leg of the life cycle dominates, Chinese and OECD gluts persist), the AI capex bust lands and leaves excess capacity plus a BIS-style demand deficit, term premium compresses to ~70, and repression is heavy at -60 bps, with a flight-to-quality bid on top. P90 = 2.95: r* at ~1.6, term premium blows out past 220 on a failed auction / basis-trade unwind, and fiscal dominance is expressed in YIELDS rather than in CAPS (repression wedge ~ -10).\nMODEL: adapter REFUSES CQ4 - \"not identified - economy-wide real rate is not a 10y sovereign real yield; no term premium\" (and grep confirms no termPremium anywhere in src/, no breakeven, no inflation swap). DIRECTIONAL CONTEXT ONLY, not adopted: the model's economy-wide real return on capital averages 5.99-6.96% across ten scenarios, with debt-populism highest at 6.955% and a 2.502 pp debt risk premium against baseline's 6.090% / 1.419 pp, while debt-populism's coupled growth falls to 1.674% from baseline 2.946%. Its internal elasticity is thus roughly -1.2 pp of growth per +100 bps of debt risk premium. I do NOT adopt that elasticity - the model has no term premium, no sovereign benchmark and no repression - but I note its SIGN agrees with my fiscal-dominance channel, and that the model's whole cross-scenario spread on the real return (~95 bps) is NARROWER than my own CQ4 P10-P90 width (180 bps), which is the right relationship, since a sovereign real yield carries term-premium and repression variance the model has no representation for at all.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.757Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) monthly average prints below 1.60% for three consecutive months, WITHOUT an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 20% in the same window (i.e. a non-flight-to-quality decline).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My explicit no-mean-reversion assumption (US 10y real averaging ~2.50% over 2032-36 against 2.60% observed Oct 30 2026) is wrong, and the secular forces I sized are cyclical after all. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.00% to ~1.50% and P10 to ~0.70%; CQ5 falls ~8 pp (a compressed term premium restores the equity-bond hedge, killing conjunct 2b); CQ6-D rises ~6 pp at B's expense (lower real rates with no growth payoff is the demand-deficit cell); CQ3 P50 rises only ~0.1 pp, since cheaper capital helps the hurdle-rate channel but does nothing for the hours or China-drag lines.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An explicit curve-management or captive-demand measure announced by any of the US, UK, Germany, France or Italy: a statutory or central-bank yield cap, a formal YCC band, or a new regulatory holding mandate covering >= 5% of that sovereign's outstanding marketable stock.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My 45% repression probability is realized rather than optional, and the 2.4 pp Debt-at-Risk sensitivity to an AI equity shock has bound. CQ4 P50 falls ~35 bps to ~1.65% and the distribution's right tail compresses (P90 to ~2.5%); CQ5 falls ~7 pp (a pinned curve cannot deliver MOVE >= 105 and restores the drawdown hedge); CQ2 RISES ~5 pp, because repression is paid for in realized inflation and the OECD core conjunct is the one that gains; CQ6-C rises ~4 pp at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment prints <= 5.0% in every quarter from 2029Q1 through 2031Q4 inclusive (12 consecutive quarters, no single-quarter exception).\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My recession add-on arithmetic (+0.50 ordinary, +0.42 deep-tail, giving a 5.72% window mean) is too pessimistic and the demographic U-lowering term is larger than the -0.3 pp I assigned. CQ1 rises from 16 to ~26 (conjunct 1 to ~58%, conjunct 2 unchanged at 22% since the fiscal-monopsony obstacle is untouched by labour tightness); CQ3 P50 rises ~0.2 pp on hours; CQ6-A rises ~4 pp. Note this signpost moves ONLY the first conjunct - it is diagnostic of my labour-market arithmetic, not of my wage arithmetic.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Reported OECD-38 + China strict operational data-centre electricity consumption for calendar year 2032 comes in at >= 900 TWh (against ~365 TWh in 2024), on a strict-operational basis comparable to the AI-Q3 definition.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033 (reporting lag allowed)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My cost-of-capital brake on AI capex is not binding, the realized AI-Q3 quantity belongs near its locked P50 of 998 rather than the P10-P25 range of 600-800 that I argued for, and the adapter's model-versus-lock datacenter disagreement resolves in the model's favour rather than mine. CQ3 P50 rises ~0.25 pp (capital deepening and TFP both); CQ6-A rises ~5 pp; CQ4 P50 rises ~20 bps, since the AI/grid capex claim on loanable funds that I haircut from +25 to +10 bps would be restored; CQ5 falls ~3 pp (a realized build means less impairment, so a smaller 2a).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Real median hourly compensation in ISIC Q (health and social work) for the OECD-38 aggregate exceeds its 2025 level by >= 8% deflated by local CPI, on a published harmonized basis.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My fiscal-monopsony claim - that care's shadow price rises but is not paid, and that the adjustment is volume rationing - has failed, and public employers have in fact outbid debt service. CQ1 rises from 16 to ~30 (conjunct 2 roughly doubles to ~42%); plank (a) rises ~10 to ~17 (the pay side of the transition works even if the matching side does not); CQ6-B rises ~5 pp at C's and D's expense (broad-based physical-sector wage gains with contained inflation is the barbell's benign leg); CQ2's OECD core conjunct rises ~4 pp, since an 8% real care-wage award at Q's ~10-11% GVA share is itself a ~0.3-0.4 pp cost-push impulse.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'rstar'."}}